WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.540
This is the single most important idea in the chapter, so hold on to it.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:13.220
Electron flow and ATP synthesis are two separate events linked only by a proton

3
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:18.000
gradient. On the left, electrons hop downhill through carriers in the inner

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.300
membrane, and each exergonic step does work by pushing protons from the matrix out

5
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.000
into the intermembrane space.

6
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.600
That accumulation of protons is potential energy, like water behind a dam.

7
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.900
On the right, ATP synthase lets those protons flood back down their gradient, and

8
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.800
that flow drives the phosphorylation of ADP.

9
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.860
Peter Mitchell proposed this in 1961, was met with deep skepticism and won the Nobel

10
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.060
Prize for being right.

11
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:50.303
Let me show you why electrons move at all.

12
00:00:50.643 --> 00:00:54.683
Every carrier has a reduction potential, plotted here on the vertical axis.

13
00:00:55.643 --> 00:01:01.103
NADH sits high, at a very negative potential, meaning it holds electrons loosely and

14
00:01:01.103 --> 00:01:02.103
donates them readily.

15
00:01:02.783 --> 00:01:08.063
Oxygen sits at the bottom, strongly positive, meaning it grabs electrons hungrily.

16
00:01:08.923 --> 00:01:14.223
Electrons always flow from low potential to high potential, from generous donor to

17
00:01:14.223 --> 00:01:19.323
greedy acceptor, and that is why they cascade from NADH down to oxygen.

18
00:01:20.003 --> 00:01:26.123
The total drop is about 1.14 volts, which translates to roughly minus 220

19
00:01:26.123 --> 00:01:27.263
kilojoules per mole.

20
00:01:27.863 --> 00:01:32.623
That released energy is exactly what gets spent pumping protons across the membrane.

21
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:36.127
Now, we assemble the chain from named parts.

22
00:01:36.807 --> 00:01:43.247
Electrons from NADH enter at Complex I, NADH Q-oxidoreductase, and are handed to

23
00:01:43.247 --> 00:01:49.847
ubiquinone. Complex III, Q-cytochrome c oxidoreductase, passes them to cytochrome c.

24
00:01:49.847 --> 00:01:55.307
Complex IV, cytochrome c oxidase, delivers them to oxygen.

25
00:01:55.827 --> 00:01:57.767
Those three are the proton pumps.

26
00:01:57.767 --> 00:01:59.427
Notice the side door.

27
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:06.627
Complex II, succinate Q reductase, feeds electrons from succinate-derived FADH2

28
00:02:06.627 --> 00:02:09.947
into the Q pool, but it does not pump protons.

29
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:16.367
That single fact, that Complex II is a feeder and not a pump, explains why FADH2

30
00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:18.887
yields less ATP than NADH.

31
00:02:19.247 --> 00:02:23.087
The reduction potentials on the left confirm every step runs downhill.

32
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:29.260
This is a beautiful piece of experimental logic, and it is a favorite exam scenario,

33
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.340
so reason through it with me.

34
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.360
If you block the chain at one point, everything upstream of the block piles up in

35
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.140
the reduced state because electrons cannot leave, and everything downstream becomes

36
00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.660
oxidized because no new electrons arrive.

37
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:49.780
So rotenone, blocking Complex I, leaves NADH reduced and the rest oxidized.

38
00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:56.280
Antimycin A, blocking Complex III, leaves carriers up to cytochrome b reduced.

39
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.200
Cyanide, blocking Complex IV, leaves the entire chain reduced and only oxygen

40
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.680
unreduced. By reading which carriers turn reduced versus oxidized, you can deduce

41
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.800
the exact sequence of electron flow.

42
00:03:10.633 --> 00:03:15.793
Tie the whole chapter together with one image, the mitochondrion as a rechargeable

43
00:03:15.793 --> 00:03:21.233
battery. The electron transport chain is the charging cable, using the energy of

44
00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:26.893
electron flow to pump protons and build up charge, the proton motive force, across

45
00:03:26.893 --> 00:03:27.933
the inner membrane.

46
00:03:28.553 --> 00:03:33.833
ATP synthase is the discharge circuit, letting protons flow back down to do useful

47
00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:35.653
work and make ATP.

48
00:03:36.453 --> 00:03:38.673
Now the diseases and drugs make sense.

49
00:03:38.673 --> 00:03:44.253
Cut the charging cable as cyanide does by blocking Complex fee and the battery never

50
00:03:44.253 --> 00:03:49.673
charges. Add an uncoupler, which pokes holes in the membrane, and the battery short

51
00:03:49.673 --> 00:03:53.293
circuits, draining its charge as heat instead of ATP.
